{"name":"Blue Zodiac","hex":"#3c4354","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"zodiac","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The belt-like region of the celestial sphere, approximately eight degrees north and south of the ecliptic, which includes the apparent path of the sun, moon, and visible planets.","The twelve equal divisions of the zodiacal region into signs or houses, each named for a prominent constellation in the region."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A inflatable boat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zodiac"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zodiac","blocks":[{"text":"The zodiac is a belt-shaped region of the sky that extends approximately 8° north and south celestial latitude of the ecliptic – the apparent path of the Sun across the celestial sphere over the course of the year. Within this zodiac belt appear the Moon and the brightest planets, along their orbital planes. The zodiac is divided along the ecliptic into 12 equal parts, called \"signs\", each occupying 30° of celestial longitude. These signs roughly correspond to the astronomical constellations with the following modern names: Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarius, and Pisces."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Ecliptic_path.jpg/330px-Ecliptic_path.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zodiac"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Zodiac_Constellations.jpg/500px-Zodiac_Constellations.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Zodiac_Constellations.jpg/500px-Zodiac_Constellations.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZodiac_Constellations.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png/1280px-Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Zodiac_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Modern zodiac wheel showing the 12 signs used in horoscopic astrology","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZodiac_(PSF).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg/500px-Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg/1280px-Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg/500px-Antoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman Egyptian coin of Antoninus Pius (dated year 8 of his reign or 145 AD) showing his portrait and a Zodiac wheel with the busts of Helios and Selene in the center","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntoninus_Pius_Drachm_showing_a_Zodiac_wheel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Beit_alfa01.jpg/500px-Beit_alfa01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Beit_alfa01.jpg/500px-Beit_alfa01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sixth-century mosaic zodiac wheel in synagogue Beth Alpha incorporating Greek-Byzantine elements, Israel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeit_alfa01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/F4.v._zodiac_circle_with_planets_-_NLW_MS_735C.png/500px-F4.v._zodiac_circle_with_planets_-_NLW_MS_735C.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/F4.v._zodiac_circle_with_planets_-_NLW_MS_735C.png/1280px-F4.v._zodiac_circle_with_planets_-_NLW_MS_735C.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse